858:
Civic space is defined as any portion of a subdivision, outside of the road and utility right-of-way) that has been set aside for the common use of the residents of the development, or for the use of the community as a whole if so designated. Common uses may include stormwater facilities, recreational amenities, or open space. If Civic Space is included within a development, it shall be in single ownership, and shall not be divided among the constituent lots of the subdivision. (Effective September 14, 2010)
610.1 Maintenance of Civic Space. The applicant shall develop a plan for ongoing maintenance any Civic Space. Such plan shall be approved by the County and filed with the Final Plat of the subdivision. The Maintenance Plan is intended to ensure the long-term maintenance of civic space and common facilities installed on a development site. This section should be used to: 1) ensure that maintenance agreements are recorded, 2) ensure that easements for maintenance and access are platted, and 3) establish maintenance requirements. The plan shall include the following information:
A. Identification of a party responsible for the ongoing ownership and maintenance of the civic space.
B. Maintenance activities for the civic space and all facilities contained therein that are part of the common elements of the development site;
C. If the responsible party is a homeowners association, the plan shall state that membership in the association shall be mandatory and automatic for all homeowners or unit owners of the development site and their successors and the plan shall be included as part of the Covenants of that development;
D. An estimate of the potential cost of the maintenance activities;
E. A statement that, to the extent permitted by law, the responsible party shall not abandoned the site or enter into voluntary dissolution (if a homeowners association) unless responsibility for the civic space is transferred to an appropriate successor.
In lieu of a maintenance plan, Brantley County may accept the dedication of any existing or future civic space for maintenance, provided that such space meets all of the requirements of this ordinance and all other applicable ordinances, is in proper working order at the time of dedication and includes adequate and perpetual access and sufficient area for access and regular maintenance. Such adequate and perpetual access shall be accomplished by granting of an easement to Brantley County or through a fee simple dedication to Brantley County. (Effective September 14, 2010)
610.2 Failure to Maintain. If the responsible party fails or refuses to meet the terms and conditions of an approved maintenance plan and/or the requirements of this ordinance, Brantley County, after thirty (30) days' written notice (except, that in the event the violation constitutes an immediate danger to public health or safety, 24 hours' notice shall be sufficient), may correct a violation by performing the work necessary to place the civic space in proper working condition. Brantley County may assess the responsible party for the cost of the repair work, which shall be a lien on the property, and may be placed on the ad valorum tax bill for such property and collected in the ordinary manner for such taxes by Brantley County. (Effective September 14, 2010)
(Ord. of 9-14-2010)
